neighbors are all dead. The loop iterates over [x,y] = A and increments counts for A's 8 neighbors. It does NOT increment A's own count. So indeed, if A has 0 live neighbors, A won't appear as a key in neighborCounts (unless some other live cell happens to be adjacent to A).\n\nWait, that IS the same thing. \"Some other live cell happens to be adjacent to A\" means A has a live neighbor. If A has 0 live neighbors, then no live cell points to A, so A isn't in neighborCounts. Correct.\n\nSo for the combined approach, we need to ensure we're handling all cases. Let me think about whether this approach works:\n\n```typescript\nconst nextGen = [...neighborCounts.entries()].flatMap(([key, count]) => {\n  const isLive = liveSet.has(key);\n  if (isLive && (count === 2 || count === 3)) return [fromKey(key)];\n  if (!isLive && count === 3) return [fromKey(key)];\n  return [];\n});\n```\n\nWait, this can be simplified:\n\n- A cell survives if it's live and has 2 or 3 neighbors\n- A cell is born if it's dead and has exactly 3 neighbors\n\nSo a cell is in the next generation if:\n- It has count 3 (regardless of live/dead status - if live, survives; if dead, born)\n- OR it has count 2 AND is live\n\n```typescript\nconst nextGen = [...neighborCounts.entries()]\n  .filter(([key, count]) => count === 3 || (count === 2 && liveSet.has(key)))\n  .map(([key]) => fromKey(key));\n```\n\nThis is elegant! It combines both conditions into one pass. Let me verify:\n- Live cell with 2 neighbors: count=2, liveSet.has(key)=true → included ✓\n- Live cell with 3 neighbors: count=3 → included ✓\n- Live cell with 1 neighbor: count=1 → excluded (dies) ✓\n- Live cell with 0 neighbors: not in neighborCounts → excluded (dies) ✓\n- Dead cell with 3 neighbors: count=3 → included (born) ✓\n- Dead cell with 2 neighbors: count=2, liveSet.has(key)=false → excluded ✓\n\nThis works! And it's more concise and reveals the logic better - a cell is alive in the next generation if it has 3 neighbors OR (2 neighbors AND currently alive).\n\nLet me calculate the mass for this approach.\n\nCurrent total: ~188\n\nNew implementation (combining survivors + births):\n```typescript\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const liveSet = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => toKey(x, y)));\n  const neighborCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n\n  for (const [x, y] of cells) {\n    for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {\n      const key = toKey(x + dx, y + dy);\n      neighborCounts.set(key, (neighborCounts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return [...neighborCounts.entries()]\n    .filter(([key, count]) => count === 3 || (count === 2 && liveSet.has(key)))\n    .map(([key]) => fromKey(key));\n}\n```\n\nChanges:\n- Removed `if (cells.length === 0) return [];` - saves: conditional -4, constants 0, [] = -2\n- Removed `survivors` computation - saves: bindings survivors, x, y, count = -4; invocations filter, get, toKey = -6; constants 0, 2, 3 = -3; loops filter = -5; conditionals ??, || = -8; assignments survivors=, count= = -12\n- Removed `births` loop - saves: bindings births, key, count = -3; constants [], 3 = -2; invocations has, push, fromKey = -6; loops for = -5; conditionals if, && = -8; assignments births=, push = -12\n- Added new combined return:\n  - Invocations: entries = 2, filter = 2, has = 2, map = 2, fromKey = 2 = 10\n  - Loops: entries iteration (via spread/entries) = hmm, filter = 5, map = 5 = 10\n  - Conditionals: || = 4, && = 4 = 8\n  - Bindings: key, count (filter) = 2, key (map) = 1 = 3\n  - Constants: 3, 2 = 2\n  - Assignment: none for const since it's a return expression\n  \nWait, the return statement with chained methods... let me think about this differently.\n\nActually, the spread `...` in `[...neighborCounts.entries()]` creates an array. But we can skip the spread and just use `Array.from()` or... actually, `neighborCounts.entries()` returns an iterable, and `.filter` and `.map` are Array methods. So we DO need to convert to array first, or we